You are supporting a process mining research prototype.
Use only the synthetic scenario information provided below.
Do not invent company-specific system names, product names, confidential logs, internal metrics, or real organizational details.
Return valid JSON only. Do not wrap it in Markdown.

Scenario ID: S06
Scenario title: Vendor Meeting and Improvement Action Follow-up
Initial workflow description:
The team meets with an equipment vendor to discuss improvement ideas. After the meeting, someone summarizes the discussion and follows up on action items.

Domain expert answers:
{
  "answers": [
    {
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "question": "What triggers the vendor meeting—is it scheduled on a recurring basis, initiated in response to a specific equipment issue, or requested ad hoc when improvement ideas arise?",
      "answer": "The meeting is initiated by either a formal request for improvement or an identified problem with equipment. An issue brief is prepared beforehand, suggesting the trigger is need-based rather than purely recurring. It is not specified whether a fixed cadence also applies."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "question": "Which internal roles (e.g., maintenance engineer, operations manager) and vendor roles are expected to participate in the meeting, and who is responsible for coordinating attendance?",
      "answer": "The core participants include a technical engineer from the equipment side, a representative from the vendor, a coordinator who arranges the session, and a supervisor who later assigns responsibilities. The coordinator handles scheduling and attendance logistics."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "question": "Does the meeting summary follow a predefined template or structure, and where is it stored and distributed after it is created?",
      "answer": "The summary captures decisions and open questions, implying a structured format. It is stored in a shared document repository and distributed by the coordinator after the meeting. Whether a formal template is mandated is not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "question": "How are action items captured and tracked after the meeting—are they logged in a specific tool, spreadsheet, or system, and how are owners and due dates assigned?",
      "answer": "Action items are recorded during the meeting and logged into a dedicated tracking tool. A supervisor reviews the list and assigns responsibility and deadlines to specific individuals. Ownership gaps are flagged as a known exception requiring resolution."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "question": "How often and through what channel does the team follow up with the vendor on open action items, and is there a defined escalation process if an item is overdue?",
      "answer": "Responsible individuals monitor progress on their assigned tasks using the tracking tool, which supports automated reminders and status updates. A missed follow-up deadline is recognized as an exception, though a formal escalation path beyond that is not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "question": "What condition marks the end of this workflow—is it when all action items are closed, when a final summary is approved by a stakeholder, or something else?",
      "answer": "The workflow concludes when all assigned tasks have been progressed and tracked to completion. Closure is tied to action-item resolution rather than a formal stakeholder sign-off, though the exact end condition is not explicitly defined beyond task tracking."
    }
  ]
}
Task: Incorporate the answers and produce an updated structured workflow representation plus automation opportunities.
Record unknown facts as remaining gaps, rather than asserting unsupported details.
Return JSON with this shape:
{
  "workflow": {
